# Example MCP client config (Streamable HTTP, v3.0.0+)

`--transport=http` exposes the same tools as stdio mode (including writes:
sending iMessage, editing contacts/calendar) over the network, so every
request must carry the bearer token from `apple-tools-mcp http-token`.

## Same machine or LAN (Claude Code, Codex, Grok Bot)

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http apple-tools http://<host>:8421/mcp \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $(apple-tools-mcp http-token)" \
  -s user
```

`<host>` is `localhost` on the same machine as the server, or the Mac
Mini's LAN hostname/IP (e.g. `peters-mac-mini.local`) from another machine
on the same network.

Grok Bot connects the same way wherever *it* runs — point its remote-MCP
config at the same URL + token; no separate per-device wiring needed.

## From an iPhone, via Tailscale (Claude / ChatGPT custom connectors)

Add as a custom connector through the web app first (neither app supports
adding a new remote MCP server from its mobile app directly — see each
app's own connector settings, which then sync to mobile):

```
URL:   http://<mini-tailscale-ip>:8421/mcp
Auth:  API key / Bearer token
Token: apple-tools-mcp http-token   (run this on the Mini to retrieve it)
```

Reachability outside your home network requires Tailscale running on both
the Mini and the phone (`tailscale status` on the Mini lists enrolled
devices). This is not exposed to the public internet — only devices in
your own tailnet can reach it.

## Getting/rotating the token

```bash
apple-tools-mcp http-token              # print the current token (generates one if missing)
```

The token lives in the macOS Keychain (service `apple-tools-mcp-http`),
never in a config file or the repo. To rotate it, delete the Keychain item
and restart the HTTP server — it'll generate a new one on the next run —
then update every client that was using the old value.
